---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditOverlayEditor from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditOverlayEditor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="edit-pdf">
  <PdfEditOverlayEditor client:load mode="full" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to edit a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above. This browser tab opens it, with no upload and no queue.</li>
        <li>Pick a tool: <strong>Text</strong>, <strong>Image</strong>, <strong>Draw</strong>, <strong>Highlight</strong>, <strong>White-out</strong>, <strong>Box</strong> or <strong>Line</strong>.</li>
        <li>Click or drag on the page. Text objects appear where you click; for the other tools, drag out a shape.</li>
        <li>Switch to <strong>Select</strong> to move objects, drag the corner handle to resize, and use the arrow keys for small moves.</li>
        <li>Press <strong>Apply</strong>, then download. Your original file on disk does not change.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What "editing a PDF" actually means</h2>
      <p>
        A PDF does not contain paragraphs. It contains a content stream that says "put this glyph at
        these coordinates in this font at this size", thousands of times per page, often out of reading
        order, and often with one word split across several instructions because of kerning. There is no
        sentence to click into and no line to reflow.
      </p>
      <p>
        Editors that offer "edit existing text" guess the paragraph structure, let you type into the
        guess, and then lay out the region again. When the guess is right, this works well. When it is
        wrong (different fonts on one line, a two-column layout, a table, a form made of underscores), it
        moves everything on the page, and you cannot undo the damage after you save.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool never corrupts a document, because it adds a layer on top. Cover the error with a
        white-out box and type the replacement. The result looks and prints correctly, and the rest of the
        page does not change by a single byte. A print shop uses the same method with a correction slip.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The seven tools</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[640px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Tool</th>
              <th scope="col">How to use it</th>
              <th scope="col">What it writes into the PDF</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Text</td>
              <td>Click, then type in the panel below</td>
              <td>Real text in a built-in font, selectable and searchable afterwards</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Image</td>
              <td>Choose a file, then drag and resize</td>
              <td>The original PNG or JPEG bytes, embedded once</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Draw</td>
              <td>Freehand with a mouse, finger or stylus</td>
              <td>A stroked polyline with round caps</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Highlight</td>
              <td>Drag across a line of text</td>
              <td>A rectangle in multiply blend mode, so words stay readable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>White-out</td>
              <td>Drag over the mistake</td>
              <td>An opaque filled rectangle</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Box</td>
              <td>Drag; toggle fill in the panel</td>
              <td>A stroked or filled rectangle at your chosen width</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Line</td>
              <td>Drag from one point to another</td>
              <td>A single stroked segment</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Rotated and cropped pages</h2>
      <p>
        Scanned documents often have a <code>/Rotate 90</code> or <code>/Rotate 270</code> entry, and many
        press-ready PDFs have a crop box smaller than their media box. Both break simple editors: your
        text is sideways, or ninety points from where you put it.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool stores each object in the displayed coordinate space of the page and converts it at
        export with the rotation and crop box of that page. Each object is exactly where the preview
        showed it, and text on a rotated page is upright.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Keyboard and accessibility</h2>
      <p>
        Each object you add can take focus. Press Tab to reach it, then use the arrow keys to move it one
        point at a time, Shift and an arrow key for ten points, and Delete or Backspace to remove it. The
        toolbar buttons are ordinary buttons, the colour swatches announce their state, and the progress
        region is announced politely while a long document is written.
      </p>
      <p>
        One point is 1/72 inch, the smallest useful adjustment in a PDF. On a touchscreen, zoom in first
        (the plus button goes to 300%); the same drag gestures work with a finger or stylus.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why nothing is uploaded</h2>
      <p>
        Server-based PDF editors upload your document, process it and send back a download link. For this
        reason they limit file sizes and daily tasks, and their privacy policies say how long your file
        stays on their disk.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page has no server. WebAssembly in your tab parses the PDF, and the edited copy is built in
        the memory of your device. To verify, open the network tab of your browser before you start, or
        turn off Wi-Fi: the tool continues to work.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
